  "image_name": "2bbb5e64d5652f20abc29fc495a0cfc8.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several ways radioactive drugs can be delivered to cancer cells through different targeting molecules at the cell surface, all aiming to induce DNA damage in the nucleus. Across the panels, the common idea is that a radioactive payload is attached to a targeting agent, which binds a membrane protein and brings damaging radiation close to the cell.\n\nOne strategy uses full antibodies that bind extracellular enzymes such as CAIX. Others use smaller ligands, including peptides or antibody fragments, to target receptors such as GPCRs or receptor tyrosine kinases like HER2. A separate approach shows small molecules binding tumor-microenvironment enzymes such as FAP. In each case, radionuclides such as ^177Lu, ^225Ac, ^212Pb, or ^90Y are depicted emitting radiation that ultimately causes nuclear DNA damage.\n\nThe figure also highlights more complex designs intended to improve targeting or potency. These include homodimerization, bispecific targeting of two different surface markers, and pretargeting, where an unlabeled antibody first binds the target and a radioactive molecule is added later. Overall, the image compares antibody-based, peptide-based, fragment-based, and small-molecule radiopharmaceutical strategies, emphasizing that different formats can reach different membrane targets while converging on the same therapeutic endpoint.",
